<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Alodia</h1><p class="st-meta">500 CE – 1504 CE (approx.) · capital Soba</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The southernmost Christian kingdom of Nubia, centred on Soba near modern Khartoum, which lasted for nearly a thousand years.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Alodia (Alwa) arose by around 500 in the region of the Blue and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-nile">White Niles</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/butana">Butana</a> plain, once the heartland of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kushite-empire">Kushite Empire</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meroe">Meroë</a>. Its capital was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soba">Soba</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blue-nile">Blue Nile</a>. Around 580 the Miaphysite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/longinus-missionary">Longinus</a>, who had earlier converted <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nobadia">Nobadia</a>, brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> to the court.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Alodia was the largest of the Nubian kingdoms, with farmland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and trade routes to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federal-democratic-republic-of-ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. The 10th-century Egyptian writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-sulaym-al-aswani">Ibn Sulaym al-Aswani</a> described it as richer and more populous than <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/makuria">Makuria</a>, with many churches at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soba">Soba</a> and a ruler governing through subordinate kings. Soba's excavated churches and imported glass and pottery confirm its wealth.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From the 12th century Alodia declined, losing territory to Arab nomads. Around 1504 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soba">Soba</a> fell to an alliance of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/abdallab-sheikhdom">Abdallab</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/funj-sultanate-of-sennar">Funj</a>, founding the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/funj-sultanate">Funj Sultanate</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> faded, though memories of Soba lived on in Sudanese tradition.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/alodia.jpg" alt="Alodia" width="320" height="238"><figcaption>Image: Dekodrak · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Possible_Flag_of_the_Kingdom_of_Alodia_(c._1350).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>500 CE – 1504 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Soba</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Miaphysite Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Nubian, Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/sudan">Sudan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Alodia, Alwa</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1002&amp;focus=alodia" aria-label="Alodia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5572 1600 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Alodia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5892 1856L5862 1746M5892 1856L5883 1851"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5862 1746h0M5883 1851h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5892 1856h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5892 1856h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Soba. <a href="/atlas?year=1002&amp;focus=alodia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
